  "body": {
    "blocking_dependencies": [
      "OSSL_provider_init -> phase 13 (providers/legacy/legacyprov.c's entry point, and the name a third-party provider's own entry point is declared with -- which is why subphase rows 6.8d and 6.12 name it, since the `providers` configuration module needs a module path and RT-PROVIDER-3P compiles its own. The authority symbol is the legacy provider's, so it is Phase 13's.)",
      "cli_option_list_parse -> phase 16 (Transferred to 22.9 and repaired there; what remains deferred to Phase 16 is regenerating the Phase-1 capture with the fixed parser. parse_option_list() (forensics/tools/atlas_runtime.py) matched the authority's `openssl list -options <cmd>` rows against a help-text shape (`-in val        Input file`) that the output never produces, and the real rows are `name type` pairs (`help -`, `in <`, `provider s`) with no leading indent, no leading dash and no description column, so every structured option_count was 0 and all 2,008 raw rows stayed unstructured. 22.9 rewrote the parser to the authority's actual grammar and re-enumerated the whole namespace from the authority's own built binary (forensics/authorities/build/openssl-3.6.4-production/apps/openssl): forensics/atlas/phase22/cli-surface.json now carries 120 commands with 4,314 structured options, 0 refused rows and 0 commands_with_zero_options, and RT-PHASE22-CLI proves the repaired parser reads a real row and refuses a malformed one (and turns to fail when the parser is broken in memory). The Phase-16-owned Phase-1 capture forensics/atlas/openssl-3.6.4-production/cli-commands.json still carries option_count 0 on all 55 commands: it is Phase 16 (cli-config) that owns the CLI stratum and would regenerate production and historical in lockstep, since regenerating only the production authority would leave a spurious option-set differential against the historical one. Closure: parse_option_list() reads the authority's actual option shape and the structured surface (22.9's cli-surface.json now, and the Phase-16 capture once regenerated) has a non-zero structured option_count for every command.)",
      "ossl_get_openssldir -> phase 16 (The OPENSSL_info strings rather than anything the registry reads; `ossl_get_enginesdir`, its sibling, moved to Phase 13.1 with `ENGINE_by_id`)",
      "ossl_get_wininstallcontext -> phase 16 (A Windows-only string the admitted Linux/x86-64 profile does not build, carried with `ossl_get_openssldir` rather than special-cased)"
    ],
